WEBVTT

00:00.050 --> 00:05.473
CAMP: Cooperative Arm–Hand Motion Planning in Constrained Spaces

00:05.658 --> 00:09.623
Constrained manipulation requires arm–hand coordination.

00:09.623 --> 00:19.330
Separate planning reduces search dimensionality but may miss feasible motions, while full joint planning faces high-dimensional search and nonconvex collision constraints.

00:19.330 --> 00:22.037
We call this the Arm–Hand Planning Dilemma.

00:22.179 --> 00:31.154
We formulate planning using feasible hand fibers: sets of hand configurations that keep the complete system collision-free for a given arm configuration.

00:31.154 --> 00:34.258
As the arm moves, these fibers change.

00:34.258 --> 00:37.445
A fixed hand shape may therefore become infeasible.

00:37.445 --> 00:42.028
Our goal is to find a continuous arm–hand path from start to goal.

00:42.473 --> 00:44.765
To find this path, we propose CAMP.

00:44.765 --> 00:49.030
CAMP first generates multiple paths in the lower-dimensional arm space.

00:49.030 --> 00:58.915
Along each guide, a layered hand search constructs a complete arm–hand candidate, while local arm relaxation adjusts both the arm and hand when the midpoint is in collision.

00:58.915 --> 01:03.730
VMP encoding provides a compact, endpoint-preserving path representation.

01:03.730 --> 01:07.158
Coarse joint optimization refines candidates in parallel.

01:07.158 --> 01:11.201
After fine refinement and decoding, we select the final trajectory.

01:11.412 --> 01:20.471
Next, we present videos of six simulation tasks, across which CAMP achieves planning success rates of 84.2–98.5%.

01:20.667 --> 01:26.471
We begin with Wall Traversal and Multi-Sphere Avoidance, which show coordinated motion around obstacles.

01:26.667 --> 01:36.473
Next, in Narrow Passage Traversal, the arm posture and hand shape change together as the system passes between the upper and lower obstacles and approaches the target configuration.

01:36.674 --> 01:39.126
We then turn to Ball-in-Box Pregrasp.

01:39.126 --> 01:44.312
The hand reaches a pregrasp pose through the opening, with no grasp performed in simulation.

01:44.699 --> 01:52.269
For Display Button Press, the hand reaches the target pose while the arm avoids the display and the adjacent barrier, without pressing the button.

01:52.680 --> 01:56.085
The final simulation task is Cabinet Bottle Pregrasp.

01:56.085 --> 02:02.288
The hand becomes compact to pass through the cabinet opening, then reopens around the bottle to reach the target pose.

02:02.667 --> 02:05.991
We also examine four representative failure cases.

02:05.991 --> 02:11.310
These examples show obstacle collisions, self-collision, and premature target collision.

02:11.310 --> 02:14.335
Red circles and labels identify each failure.

02:14.541 --> 02:18.313
Then, we present videos of the real-robot experiments.

02:19.000 --> 02:22.651
We begin the real-robot experiments with Ball-in-Box Grasp.

02:22.651 --> 02:26.825
The hand reconfigures as the arm guides it through the square opening toward the ball.

02:28.100 --> 02:34.289
After reaching the target, the grasp and lift follow a manually adjusted trajectory for demonstration.

02:36.272 --> 02:45.024
Next, in Display Button Press, the hand reconfigures in advance to avoid the display and the adjacent barrier, reaching the target pose without pressing the button.

02:49.206 --> 02:54.391
Finally, in Cabinet Bottle Grasp, the hand becomes compact to pass through the barrier opening.

02:56.300 --> 02:59.761
We manually add hand closure and lifting for demonstration.
